 ### [Hardware-assisted Volume Deformation](9864-hardware-assisted-volume-deformation.md)
 Hardware-assisted volume deformation renders non-linearly deformed volumes by integrating the deformation process directly into the fragment shader, avoiding the computational expense of pre-computing a resampled deformed volume. Unlike methods that pre-compute the deformed volume (e.g., [LGL95]), this approach allows for dynamic deformations at interactive rates by evaluating the inverse deformation function per-pixel. It builds on [Hardware-Accelerated and GPU Implementations](9525-hardware-accelerated-and-gpu-implementations.md) by modifying the texture coordinate generation and sampling logic to account for spatial transformations.
 
-#### GPU-based Tiled Ray Casting with Depth Peeling
-- **Definition**: A ray-casting algorithm for time-varying scalar fields on unstructured meshes, implemented using DirectX 9.0c, which uses depth peeling to handle non-convex meshes [S1, unofficial].
-- **Comparison**: Due to GPU memory limitations, this method could only run on smaller datasets compared to the HAVS method [S1, unofficial].
+### [Hardware-Accelerated AMR Volume Rendering via Homogenization](10503-hardware-accelerated-amr-volume-rendering-via-homogenization.md)
+An interactive DVR technique for AMR data that subdivides the dataset into regions of constant resolution using an AMR Partition Tree (generalized kD-tree) and renders regions using hardware-accelerated DVR.
 
-#### Brick Boundary Clamping
-- **Definition**: A technique to clamp the last ray sample position to the volume or brick extents to prevent artifacts like holes in isosurfaces caused by tri-linear interpolation at brick boundaries [S2].
-- **Step**: Clip each ray against the boundaries of the volume/brick using a method similar to Sutherland-Hodgman polygon clipping to find the intersection with the back face. Repeat the clipping process for the y- and z-axes to yield the final intersection of the ray and the back face [S2].
-- **Code**: The following fragment program code clamps the last ray sample position to the volume or brick extents for the x-axis:
-  ```assembly
-  MOV scale, 0.0;
-  # Test for positions outside the extents
-  SUBC temp.x, volExtentMax, pos;
-  MOV scale.x (LT), temp.x;
-  SUBC temp.x, pos, volExtentMin;
-  MOV scale.x (LT), temp.x;
-  # Determine correction vector
-  DIV temp.x, scale.x, offset.x;
-  ABS temp.x, temp.x;
-  # Move back to max/min x-axis extent
-  MAD pos, -temp.x, offset, pos;
-  ```
-- **Pitfall**: Using one voxel overlap between bricks and correct last sampling points is insufficient when using pre-integration because different sampling distances occur. This is overcome by using incremental pre-integration tables as presented by Weiler et al. [WKME03] [S2].
+### [GPU Based Single-Pass Raycasting](10854-gpu-based-single-pass-raycasting.md)
+GPU Based Single-Pass Raycasting is a volume rendering technique where the volume is traversed along a single ray per pixel, implemented within the Volume Rendering Component framework. It solves the limitations of polygonal-based methods (which are limited in expressive power) and slicing techniques (which can lead to artifacts) by providing high-quality visual results. It is distinguished from its siblings by its specific implementation as a single-pass fragment shader execution on modern graphics hardware, relying on hardware-accelerated 3D texture sampling for trilinear interpolation.
+
+### Brick Boundary Clamping
+#### definition
+- [S1] A technique to clamp the last ray sample position to the volume or brick extents to prevent artifacts like holes in isosurfaces caused by tri-linear interpolation at brick boundaries.
+#### step
+- [S1] Clip each ray against the boundaries of the volume/brick using a method similar to Sutherland-Hodgman polygon clipping to find the intersection with the back face.
+- [S1] Repeat the clipping process for the y- and z-axes to yield the final intersection of the ray and the back face.
+#### code
+- [S1] The following fragment program code clamps the last ray sample position to the volume or brick extents for the x-axis:
+```assembly
+MOV scale, 0.0;
+# Test for positions outside the extents
+SUBC temp.x, volExtentMax, pos;
+MOV scale.x (LT), temp.x;
+SUBC temp.x, pos, volExtentMin;
+MOV scale.x (LT), temp.x;
+# Determine correction vector
+DIV temp.x, scale.x, offset.x;
+ABS temp.x, temp.x;
+# Move back to max/min x-axis extent
+MAD pos, -temp.x, offset, pos;
+```
+#### pitfall
+- [S1] Using one voxel overlap between bricks and correct last sampling points is insufficient when using pre-integration because different sampling distances occur. This is overcome by using incremental pre-integration tables as presented by Weiler et al. [WKME03].
+
+### LockSampleDistanceToInputSpacing
+#### definition
+- [S2, unofficial] A correction technique to equalize the ray sampling distance used by GPU and CPU mappers to reduce sampling seams. It involves enabling the `LockSampleDistanceToInputSpacing` mode in the GPU mapper, which was previously only available in the CPU mapper (`vtkFixedPointVolumeRayCastMapper`).
+#### step
+- [S2, unofficial] Enable the `LockSampleDistanceToInputSpacing` internal mode in the GPU mapper to decrease the sampling distance passed by `vtkSmartVolumeMapper`, equalizing it with the CPU mapper.
+#### comparison
+- [S2, unofficial] Before this correction, the CPU mapper's mode decreased sampling distance making artifacts more evident in the GPU mapper. After enabling it in the GPU mapper, seams faded out but remained visible at block-edge planes.
 
 ## Background
-The implementation of volume rendering on graphics hardware emerged in the early 2000s as GPU capabilities evolved to support programmable shaders and 3D texture units. Early work focused on adapting the ray casting algorithm to the fixed-function pipeline using texture combiners and multi-pass techniques to handle state accumulation [7443-volume-ray-casting-on-graphics-hardware.md]. As programmable shaders became standard, the focus shifted to single-pass fragment shader implementations that leverage hardware texture interpolation for efficiency [5616-hardware-accelerated-volume-rendering.md]. Recent developments include browser-based implementations using WebGL for interactive visualization [8592-webgl-path-tracing-volume-rendering.md] and specialized techniques for handling dynamic data and deformations [9864-hardware-assisted-volume-deformation.md][S1, unofficial].
+The shift from software-based ray casting to hardware-accelerated implementations was driven by the need for real-time interaction with large volumetric datasets. Early implementations leveraged the fixed-function texture hardware of graphics cards to perform trilinear interpolation, which was significantly faster than CPU-based interpolation [5616-hardware-accelerated-volume-rendering.md]. As programmable shaders became available, more complex optical models, lighting, and acceleration techniques like early ray termination could be integrated directly into the rendering pipeline [7443-volume-ray-casting-on-graphics-hardware.md]. Modern implementations continue to evolve, supporting complex data structures like AMR and unstructured meshes, as well as advanced features like path tracing in web environments [8592-webgl-path-tracing-volume-rendering.md][10503-hardware-accelerated-amr-volume-rendering-via-homogenization.md].
 
